  "worked_solution": "First, let's make a sketch of our region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100085/assets/c8cf45693dbf89b4f2b5efda32904d07d8205770419cc5ffdec63f4b43c0e145.png)\n\nNow, let's represent $D$ as a type I region. \n\n- To determine the $x$-limits, we need to find the $x$-coordinates of the points at which the curves intersect. So, we set the following equation and solve it for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2} & =2x \\\\ {x}^{2}-2x & =0 \\\\ x(x-2) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $x$-limits are \n$0\\le x\\le 2.$\n\n- To determine the lower and upper functions, we draw a vertical arrow through the region.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14100085/assets/14d0f60200b6efee0de1611a51a71e27eb6070697cb02db58007b52a8b3f172d.png)\n\nAs a result, we get\n\n$$\nD={(x,y)\\,:\\,0\\le x\\le 2,\\,{x}^{2}\\le y\\le 2x}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can express the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\u222c}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}f(x,y)\\text{\u00a0}dy\\,dx \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2}[{\\int }_{{x}^{2}}^{2x}f(x,y)\\text{\u00a0}dy]dx\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
